westerns have been around for over a  century which means the genre is full of  pits and misses so how can you find the  very best of the best  well we've got you covered because these  are some of the westerns you need to see  before you die  clint eastwood director sergio leone  didn't invent the spaghetti western but  they pretty much perfected it with the  good the bad and the ugly  leone's eye for beautiful shots and his  ability to film brutal intense action  scenes  aided of course by enyo moriconi's  amazing musical score makes this film  stand out as a master class in creating  a simple  plot that means a great deal eastwood  plays the man with no name  although he's referred to as blondie  throughout the film he and two other  gunslingers are searching for a lost  stockpile of confederate gold  throughout the movie they keep running  into each other gleaning more and more  information about the whereabouts of  their prize until they finally arrive at  their destination  what follows is a three-way duel for all  the marbles with plenty of twists and  intense stare downs  spaghetti westerns are a great  demonstration of how our genre can adapt  and evolve over  time if you like the good the bad and  the ugly check out the other eastwood  leone team-ups  a fistful of dollars and for a few  dollars more all of these films are  classics that deserve a lot of praise  bravo it's one of the best buddy  westerns of all time  you can't go wrong with robert redford  and paul newman in butch cassidy and the  sundance kid  it's a movie where almost from the  get-go you can tell that disaster is  inevitable for our leading men  regardless it never gets old watching  the two struggle with their attempts to  escape the long-reaching arm of the law  all while cracking jokes and being  undeniably cool the story goes like this  butch is the charismatic leader of a  gang and sundance is his trusty  lieutenant  after a score goes wrong the two realize  they need to escape from the mobilized  lawmen who are after them  their solution is to flee to south  america along with sundance's girlfriend  where they'll be able to continue their  crime spree unimpeded  it's a fairly bare bones plot but the  star power of the two leads is magnetic  you really root for butch and sundance  to figure things out and find the life  they're searching for  and it seems like it just may happen  right up until it doesn't the finale is  a massive standoff against a literal  army of soldiers with a closing shot  you'll have a hard time forgetting  the late 1980s and early 1990s saw a  nice little revival for the western  genre with films like silverado and  dances with wolves  however one of the best of the bunch has  to be 1993's tombstone  it's a veritable who's who of some of  the biggest actors of the time  you've got val kilmer sam elliott bill  paxton and kurt russell among many  others  it's also immensely quotable mostly due  to a career defining performance from  kilmer as doc holliday  a movie sidekick who doesn't get nearly  enough credit you're probably seeing  double  i have two guns one for each of you  at first glance tombstone reads an awful  lot like a big dumb action movie but the  truth behind the film is actually pretty  fascinating  although it's obviously been dramatized  most of the events that take place in  the movie really occurred in the town of  tombstone arizona  there's the shootout at the o.k corral  wyatt earp's miraculous stand at the  creek ambush and the final ride to  destroy the cowboys  in fact some of the most ridiculous  aspects of the film are reportedly what  truly happened  some people might dismiss tombstone as a  pop western but don't fall into that  trap  just because it stars a lot of popular  90s actors doesn't mean it's a lesser  movie  regarded by many as sergio leone's best  spaghetti western once upon a time in  the west does a brilliant job of  matching impressive substance a leone's  well-known style  clint 