 developing his classic film taxi  driver starring robert de niro  the assassination of jesse james by the  coward robert ford has many strengths  including its impressive cast it  generally plays as a quiet  character-driven drama but it's  punctuated by loud violent acts that  keep the plot rolling  brad pitt and casey affleck star and it  also features sam rockwell and jeremy  renner  sadly this underrated film didn't  perform very well at the box office and  that's a real tragedy because it's a  genuine masterpiece  the film is based on a novel by ron  hansen and it follows james gang in the  events that eventually lead up to his  killing  as the members of his gang start to grow  distrustful and stab one another in the  back jesse keeps searching for men he  can trust  and puts his faith in the wrong person  one of the best aspects of the film is  how the aftermath of james's death is  handled  ford expects to receive a massive reward  and gain celebrity status for doing the  deed  instead he's reviled by anyone who knows  and the government never follows through  with the bounty  the assassination of jesse james does as  good a job as any westerner showing that  the glamorized ideals of the wild west  fall far  short of reality it may not seem cutting  edge nowadays but the wild bunch was a  fairly revolutionary film in 1969 both  for its cinematography and its subject  matter  it was nominated for several awards  across a variety of categories earning  accolades for its writing cinematography  and direction  the film was also controversial as the  violence was extremely graphic for a  mainstream film at the time  plus the main characters for the most  part are inherently unlikable  if they move kill them like many  westerns the wild bunch focuses on a  group of men trying to find their place  in a changing world  it tells the story of a gang of aging  outlaws who get entangled in several  difficult conflicts as they try to stay  alive and find their fortune  